When you define a type, you can use a union to indicate that the type can be one of multiple types. For example, the type of a variable that can be either a string or a number could be defined as:

```typescript
type NameOrNumber = string | number;
```

In this case, the type system knows that the value could be either a string or a number, but it doesn't know which one it will be. However, if you have additional information about the value, such as the fact that it will always be a string, you can use a type assertion to tell the type system this information. A type assertion is a way of telling the type system that you know something about the type of a value that it doesn't, and it's usually used when you have a value that is of a type that is more general than you need it to be.

For example, if you have a variable that is of type `NameOrNumber`, but you know that it will always be a string, you can use a type assertion to tell the type system this information:

```typescript
let name: NameOrNumber = "John Doe";
// name is of type NameOrNumber, but it will always be a string

let name2: string = name;
// name2 is of type string, since we know that name will always be a string
```

In this case, the type system knows that `name` is of type `NameOrNumber`, but we use a type assertion to tell it that we know that it will always be a string. This allows us to assign `name` to a variable of type `string` without any errors.

Note that type assertions are often used as a workaround for situations where the type system is too restrictive, but they can also be used to improve readability and clarity of code. It's important to use them judiciously and only when necessary, as they can make code less type-safe and more prone to errors.

